#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162856} :activity/id #uuid "c9eb15d5-cbd1-5141-92c1-c7ab44b90c6f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049750 :topic/id #uuid "10d700a2-07da-5c7f-b46c-fcc2c6f157f6" :topic/math-academy-id 3129} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162856 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163231 :tutorial/content "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix with real entries. As we know, there exists an orthonormal change of variables \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=P\\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nthat transforms the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^TA\\mathbf{x}$ into the quadratic form $\\mathbf{y}^TD\\mathbf{y},$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix. \n\nThe columns of the matrix $P$ are the (normalized) unit eigenvectors of $A,$ which are mutually orthogonal. These are called the **principal axes** of the quadratic form.\n\nLet's use this knowledge to find out which curve is defined by the equation \n\n$$\n6x_1^2+4x_1x_2+3x_2^2= 14,\n$$\n\nand see the geometric interpretation of the principal axes.\n\nIf we plot our curve using a graphical calculator, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/d8/d8a6b6dff52e0c4004432f9447b5fd484612eaacf07588c3469af143b0a9e880.png)\n\nIt looks like an ellipse. Let's now find our coordinate system, in which the equation of the curve will be the simplest.\n\nFirst, we consider the corresponding quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})={\\color{blue}6}x_1^2+{\\color{red}4}x_1x_2+{\\color{blue}3}x_2^2,\n$$\n\nwhich we reduce to its canonical (diagonal) form. \n\nSo, we write down the matrix of $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/2a/2a4229ed50ef304e06cec6a64a3fa522a0aa679b70ebc1608b9042aedbbed2ef.svg)\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +14 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =7,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the principal axes of the curve.\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/85/85e44a8dc5afeda8a266dca013f4298e7e475ea6ce7808cfe3072b4b23186334.svg) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{5},$ we get \n![](images/81/81c9b8b9f946e7c427bae2c1a7efa0a57c9f0388c9d67d846a14971bf9f33902.svg)\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/d6/d62eeed8be7829fc1d145697fb700d5f282ceaf45c3d967a8cb1270b4399f906.svg) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{5},$ we get \n![](images/61/6123f9b7e7cce970066dea2ab36a4e1d2704a17af961d03e016d83683eb13124.svg)\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our quadratic form (and the corresponding curve) are\n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/f1/f10b4edf9545e958d4f32a695da26077c1ab7baf096c0956f1e02ba9ec94c8bd.svg) and ![](images/04/04a614acc9a6be123cdd1a8c765853a822da12f07137baaefef9f4715c59a5f3.svg)\n\nNotice that in the diagram below, the principal axes correspond to the axes $y_1$ and $y_2.$ \n\n![](images/10/10a2f0ccd179eba7f65410f932548d954a459e1bc8dcb0803a5b2d9fe7896432.png)\n\nIn the new coordinate system, our quadratic form can be reduced to \n\n$$\n7y_1^2 + 2y_2^2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2} & =14 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt2$ and $\\sqrt{7}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2f9d7d81-cc50-5024-8f69-3b529968aeac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4890 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d3ecee7b-206f-5bb5-9257-61e0758f2151" :step/math-academy-id 30544 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162923}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162900 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162011 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161803 :question/id #uuid "42777bd2-44d0-5117-9e8b-ee722d8f3229" :question/math-academy-id "e-8276"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6832be-62e1-506c-9b78-685f65362117" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552513}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve"} :step/id #uuid "51f3b5b0-1a5d-56cf-a16d-5c612010bfbf" :step/math-academy-id 30924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162901 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162012 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161804 :question/id #uuid "3564c452-1a51-5455-a6b1-223fda1611c0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8277"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5d6ac06-40b4-5da0-bd26-860b35826292" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049174}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552487}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Plane Curve"} :step/id #uuid "0d0b9890-4620-5b89-b7da-8090ecf6f057" :step/math-academy-id 30925 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162938}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162923 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162023 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161815 :question/id #uuid "ec194ea7-df30-5370-b0c4-d27d4b34f8c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8473"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf2e573c-f351-5d5a-8e59-9c6532f1ab9d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049456}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552466}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Curve Using a Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "25baa4e8-cd30-578f-b8a2-6f5442b1bca7" :step/math-academy-id 31528 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162901}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162938 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163256 :tutorial/content "In some special cases, a quadratic equation might generate a degenerate curve or even an empty set.\n\n- For example, consider the quadratic equation \n\n$$\n-3x_1^2+8x_1x_2+3x_2^2 = 0.\n$$\n\nReducing the left-hand side to the canonical form, we obtain\n\n$$\n5y_1^2 - 5y_2^2 = 0\n$$\n\nwhich represents two intersecting lines $y_1 = y_2$ and $y_1 = -y_2.$\n\n- Now consider the quadratic equation \n\n$$\n-2x_1^2+8x_1x_2-8x_2^2 = 10.\n$$\n\nReducing the left-hand side to the canonical form, we obtain\n\n$$\n- 10y_2^2 =10\n$$\n\nwhich represents an empty set as the equation $y_2^2 = -1$ does not have any real solution.\n\n- Finally, reducing the left-hand side of \n\n$$\nx_1^2-4x_1x_2+4x_2^2 = 5\n$$\n\nto the canonical form, we obtain\n\n$$\n5y_2^2 =5\n$$\n\nwhich represents a pair of parallel lines $y_2 = 1$ and $y_2=-1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cf07f443-3757-578e-908a-51eaef4cde16" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5077 :tutorial/title "Some Special Cases"} :step/id #uuid "2d3c6630-85aa-524d-9982-1c2a440c6b5f" :step/math-academy-id 31799 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162949}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162949 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162044 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161838 :question/id #uuid "ba46e82f-10b0-529c-94b0-3aebb7d1e1a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8678"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e97b8e36-3790-5cdc-bd77-417574af23f3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570028}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve Given the Corresponding Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "ab944e55-c021-5455-a8c9-8144173eabb7" :step/math-academy-id 32221 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162900}}] :activity/title "Reducing a Quadratic Curve to Its Principal Axes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162224}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411465} :activity/id #uuid "5c959c8a-bb4d-57d5-8ba1-a3ae664b0bd0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049750 :topic/id #uuid "10d700a2-07da-5c7f-b46c-fcc2c6f157f6" :topic/math-academy-id 3129} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411463 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162668 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c050d04-cc57-5e4f-9981-5ac992758bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411430} {:answer/id #uuid "352606af-80ab-5092-8c74-0764cad932bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411434} {:answer/id #uuid "72d0140b-00e2-525a-ace5-a15c912b0d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411439} {:answer/id #uuid "a251106f-0d50-5a25-bdd3-04521faa8cc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411447} {:answer/id #uuid "ba35334a-83ae-5683-b824-2a6a574c3bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411448}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411448} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbf54027-1555-5e76-9f5a-c93113c1e7f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ec22f795-26e2-5bc9-b423-bed373ca22ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-95809" :question/problem "Find the principal axes of the curve ${x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=48.$" :question/worked-solution "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=48\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "19ff91ae-0eac-5f3c-95d1-bb03c7aa3351" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411467}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411464 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24fa485f-91ea-5bd7-b5bf-ff53f7921b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A  hyperbola with semiaxes  $2$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411433} {:answer/id #uuid "40462880-f937-54f8-9c63-a892cafb32da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes  $2$ and $\\sqrt{8}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411435} {:answer/id #uuid "69cb5485-7267-5d3c-8e9b-6410002fdbd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes  $\\sqrt{8}$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411438} {:answer/id #uuid "a1267461-8286-578d-8e73-1197cd603858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A circle with radius  $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411446} {:answer/id #uuid "efcf49e3-8778-553a-9158-db5800030dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Two parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411453}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411433} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fdaee3e-396b-5d40-981f-615ed5526c05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6abf962b-d107-56b7-9263-80678639c14a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95002" :question/problem "Which curve is defined by the equation $3{x}_{1}^{2}+10{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}=32?$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+10{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.  \n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 5 & 3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda -16 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =8,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $8{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}.$  \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2} & =32 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes  $2$ and $4.$"} :step/id #uuid "226f8cb7-5c22-5a4b-8d5c-99f40fa38b6f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411466}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411465 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "645a7e0b-2e3d-5867-b381-6a1443ab619a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411437} {:answer/id #uuid "87d10ffd-ae37-597e-8619-29681e2d4321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411442} {:answer/id #uuid "cc8968ee-c0f9-5a40-a746-8ba2c518a9ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411451} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0e0014-393a-5d8a-a0b7-5361c1d979fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411452} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e73c4f-2431-51f3-9280-279505b4a83a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411454}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411442} :answer-field/id #uuid "66f12865-3f75-50a9-82af-d153989de8e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f8d1da1f-2237-5249-8b88-e74004bb86c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94985" :question/problem "Which curve is defined by the equation $9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}=10?$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.  \n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}9-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-15\\lambda +50 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $5{y}_{1}^{2}+10{y}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}+10{y}_{2}^{2} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{1} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{1} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $1.$"} :step/id #uuid "8ca779b8-f452-5804-afcb-78fa42b0b171" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411464}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411466 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411461 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "225ec156-2776-5590-b42c-ad1225863eb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411432} {:answer/id #uuid "84e45c48-61da-5ecf-bca7-7e74358b27b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411441} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0771fd-b582-50d2-908c-56e97b6c3960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411443} {:answer/id #uuid "97642761-88ff-5804-a356-24637c6c8d56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411445} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b9b25d-c9bb-564c-9102-d912c2e151d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411450}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411432} :answer-field/id #uuid "b632b82e-8e97-55cb-a0fa-eef141821277" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/re