d #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8e9c9346-9c39-50ca-b199-7d5f576a50dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-94678" :question/problem "Find the principal axes of the curve $2x_{1}^{2} - 4x_{1}x_{2} + 5x_{2}^{2} = 1$."} :step/id #uuid "7148b6dc-5462-53d7-b362-ba92302a97f2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570088}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570028 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a0dc931-f995-5347-aa14-643adee1237e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569483} {:answer/id #uuid "23d8244b-8a6a-5c2f-8ca7-f1e17fd9fd2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569492} {:answer/id #uuid "41a1b312-a4c9-5ed8-a753-89475be0fb25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569534} {:answer/id #uuid "50a60166-a4d0-5973-950e-e9d7415fe849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569561} {:answer/id #uuid "bf3682ac-7215-584a-b7a8-cde01af0ad65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569749}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569483} :answer-field/id #uuid "a323c846-4657-566e-a5fd-187d291c9d9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d4dcf09b-7fd5-51e9-80d9-32152841ce22" :question/math-academy-id "q-97989" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=3.$ Given that $A$ has  eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ find the principal axes of the curve." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=3\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "bf221691-b747-5306-b82b-6ad8ac169966" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570090}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45c1b6a5-4b25-5add-933a-f859d06c77a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404824} {:answer/id #uuid "30a5ae97-cd39-55ef-a5bf-80065a90ea68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404825} {:answer/id #uuid "09570439-79f9-5675-832c-49fd5c55e309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404826} {:answer/id #uuid "0f25e08a-9cf2-5edf-928f-b5b9f6598ec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404827} {:answer/id #uuid "c035c04d-ee78-561a-a75e-0660a545532b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404828}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404826} :answer-field/id #uuid "03775e48-d8c6-5bdb-abb2-c177de91a6fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0fc392a3-f500-5b88-9a2e-df6cdd3a73e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-95805" :question/problem "Find the principal axes of the curve $9{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-6{x}_{2}^{2}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=9{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-6{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=5\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=10\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-7.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-7)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}+7\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "c1373035-5cf1-5943-b35e-0caec7a87e03" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570086}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570090 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a252908-6cba-57b3-9ed0-dd7e95aa69c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405176} {:answer/id #uuid "c85e28c4-0aa8-5374-afe0-82128df5ed7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405177} {:answer/id #uuid "12ee5128-654b-5538-a73f-170cdd7589be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405178} {:answer/id #uuid "5810134f-edb2-5145-b526-fe3657df320f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405179} {:answer/id #uuid "16848eb1-6562-5462-b01d-8bdf402e1e48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405180}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405177} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0d2f6ab-2224-5973-9cb7-233b0a4ecb90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5fb95d9e-0ced-5515-baa2-9595c0717556" :question/math-academy-id "q-97990" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=7.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ find unit vectors along the principal axes of the curve corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}$, respectively." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=7\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"} :step/id #uuid "c5bef920-5869-56ae-ae31-b6c165811695" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570096}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570096 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25ce6f39-b554-5187-a596-bea3e0d74a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{6}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269604} {:answer/id #uuid "641003f6-61b5-5644-8014-b7211b838154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $6$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270158} {:answer/id #uuid "800353a6-8373-5ac8-bef8-56fc85f12c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{6}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270382} {:answer/id #uuid "87164931-280e-5c45-aacc-71befec81b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Two parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270439} {:answer/id #uuid "9ed379e0-160e-54b7-bbb9-f002a3aca592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $6$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270675}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269604} :answer-field/id #uuid "d36fcb91-df23-5bd5-8ac0-2f8bab67002c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fe9938a7-e3f2-57da-8c02-e91f20cff686" :question/math-academy-id "q-140549" :question/problem "Which curve is defined by the equation $3{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}=24?$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & -5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -24 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $4{y}_{1}^{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}_{1}^{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2} & =24 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{6}$ and $2.$"} :step/id #uuid "ef0fa292-195b-5d7e-9069-13a2db1d342f"}] :activity/title "Reducing a Quadratic Curve to Its Principal Axes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569432}]}
